  "bibliography": {
    "abstract": "This study examines whether stakeholder-prioritized governance needs and key performance indicators (KPIs) for shared mobility are similar between Europe and the Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC). Understanding these similarities is important for assessing whether governance approaches developed in established mobility markets can inform regulatory oversight in emerging mobility environments. A modified Delphi Poll including surveys followed by Semi-Structured Interviews was conducted with mobility experts from both regions. Survey responses were analyzed using the Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP), and interview data was examined through reflexive thematic analysis. Findings show substantial alignment in operational governance priorities, particularly regarding safety, service reliability, and accessibility compliance. However, qualitative results indicate that differences in regulatory maturity, institutional capacity, and urban operating environments influence how KPIs are interpreted and implemented in practice. These findings suggest that governance frameworks for shared and mobility may be adaptable across regions at a strategic level, while requiring contextual adjustment in application.",
