  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely on qualitative interviews and subjective expert rankings rather than empirical measurements of policy outcomes or operational data.",
      "what": "This study compared governance priorities and key performance indicators (KPIs) for shared and micromobility between Europe and the Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC) using a modified Delphi Poll, surveys, and semi-structured interviews with regional mobility experts. Survey data was analyzed using the Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP) alongside reflexive thematic analysis of the interviews. The results show strong alignment on operational priorities like safety, reliability, and accessibility, but reveal that differences in regulatory maturity and institutional capacity alter how these KPIs are implemented in practice.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that while strategic-level governance frameworks for shared mobility can be adapted across highly different regional contexts, the actual application of regulatory oversight requires localized adjustment rather than direct policy copying."
    }
